 # Alerts that deserve to wake somebody
 
-A queue's numbers are [<!-- TODO: link the module that exports them -->](TODO), and they are reported
-as one line per queue every time a run finishes saying what it did. This page is what the deployment
-does with them, and it has to be written the way an alert has to be written to be worth having: each
-rule names the signal it reads, the threshold that fires it, the window the threshold is measured
-over, and the drill that triggers it on purpose.
+A queue's numbers are [five counters and one gauge](../src/service/signals.rs), cut by queue, and
+they are reported as one line per queue every time a run finishes saying what it did. This page is
+what the deployment does with them, and it is written the way an alert has to be written to be worth
+having: each rule names the signal it reads, the threshold that fires it, the window the threshold
+is measured over, and the drill that triggers it on purpose.
+
+A rule that leaves any of those out is a rule nobody can review. And a rule whose signal is not in
+the table is worse than that: it is a rule that never fires, and it looks exactly like a healthy
+queue.
 
 ## What we promise
 
-The budget: <!-- TODO: the share of settled attempts that has to finish the job, and how many settled
-attempts a window needs before its share is read at all. Say both as numbers in this section — the
-spec compares them with `Budget::standard()` in `src/service/signals.rs`, so a page that says
-something else is a page that has stopped being true. -->
+**99% of the attempts that settle finish their job.** An *attempt that settles* is one that ended —
+finished, failed, or was reclaimed by the store — and it is the unit the budget is measured in
+rather than the job that arrived or the attempt that started: a queue whose submissions stopped has
+a failure rate of zero and no work at all, and a job that is still being retried has not been
+judged yet.
+
+A window is read once **at least 100 attempts** have settled in it. Below that, a share is a
+statement about one unlucky job and not about a queue — one failure in one attempt is a 100%
+failure rate, and it is also a Tuesday. The floor is part of the rule rather than a habit of the
+person reading it.
 
-The unit is the attempt that *settled*: finished, failed, or reclaimed. A retried attempt is the
-budget working; an attempt nobody finished is a loss no failure counter in this crate sees.
+What the two halves of the arithmetic are worth remembering for: a **failed attempt that is retried
+is the budget working** — the engine spends an attempt and gets a delivery — while a **reclaimed
+attempt is a loss that no failure counter in this crate sees** ([the worker](../src/service/worker.rs)
+hands back what it cannot finish, and the store reclaims what a dead worker left). A window that
+loses every job it claims has a failure counter of zero.
 
 ## Rules
 
 | signal | threshold | window | drill | severity | what to do |
 | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-
-<!-- TODO: one row per family `signals()` exports, at least. The signal column is a name from that
-table — a rule written against a number nobody counts is a rule that never fires. The drill column is
-the command that triggers the rule on purpose; it is `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts`, which
-is also what the spec checks for. -->
+| `jobq_failed_total` | more than 1% of the attempts that settled in the window | `5m` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | page | The fast burn. One queue is failing faster than its budget allows, right now. Read its attempt lines for `outcome=failed`, group them by `reason`, and fix or reject the payloads behind the biggest group; a job that has spent its budget is a dead letter ([the view the operator uses next](../src/engine/dlq.rs)). |
+| `jobq_failed_total` | more than 1% of the attempts that settled in the window | `1h` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | ticket | The slow burn: the same share, held for an hour, is what a fast window misses during a trickle of traffic. It is a ticket rather than a page because the fast rule above will have paged already if the queue is actually on fire. |
+| `jobq_reclaimed_total` | 2 or more in the window | `5m` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | page | Work nobody finished: a worker stopped holding a job, or a lease ran out. Nobody reported a failure, so this is the alert that exists because no other one fires. Start from the `worker` field of the attempt lines and the exit reason of that pod; a restart loop is the usual cause, and every reclaim is an attempt the job will never get back. |
+| `jobq_reclaimed_total` | 20 or more in the window | `1h` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | ticket | The same loss at the rate a fleet that restarts on a schedule produces. Nobody is paged, because nothing is down — what is being spent is the retry budget of jobs that were fine. |
+| `jobq_running` | at the batch size (8 by default) for the whole window | `15m` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | page | Workers are holding everything they are allowed to hold and finishing nothing. Either a handler is stuck (the attempt lines for those jobs stop after the claim) or the store stopped answering; this is the one number that rises when the queue is stuck rather than failing. |
+| `jobq_claimed_total` | 0 while the submitted total keeps rising | `15m` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | page | Jobs are arriving and nobody is taking them. Either the workers are gone, or every one of them is refusing the queue — both are invisible in the failure counters, because nothing has failed yet. |
+| `jobq_succeeded_total` | 0 rise while the claimed total keeps rising | `15m` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | page | The other half of a stuck queue, and the one an operator checks first: claims are being spent and nothing is finishing. Read the `took_ms` of the attempt lines — work that takes longer than the lease is work that will be reclaimed next. |
+| `jobq_submitted_total` | 0 for a queue that had traffic | `1h` | `cargo test -p jobq --test spec_alerts` | ticket | The producer is broken, or its credentials expired. This is the only rule here about a number that is too *quiet*, and it is a ticket rather than a page because a queue that is draining happily is not an incident for the queue. |
 
 ## Escalation
 
-<!-- TODO: what to do while the cause is unknown, and the one instruction that is counter-intuitive
-about a queue that lives in a store. -->
+When the queue is losing work and nobody knows why yet: stop the bleed before diagnosing it. The
+budget a job is given is a setting, and raising it ([`worker`](../src/service/config.rs)) turns a
+queue that is losing jobs into a queue that is retrying them — the attempts are cheap and the dead
+letters are not. What it does not do is fix anything, and it spends the same budget the rules above
+are watching.
+
+The one thing not to do is restart the daemon. The queue lives in the store: a restart releases
+what this worker holds (an attempt nobody finished) and the next worker picks the job up, which is
+correct and also exactly what the reclaim counter just told you about.
 
 ## Where this comes from
 
-<!-- TODO: the pages this one is built on — an alerting-rules reference, and the burn-rate framing
-the two windows come from. -->
+- [Prometheus: alerting rules](https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/alerting_rules/)
+  — the `for:` clause and the expression syntax the windows here are written for;
+- [Google SRE workbook: alerting on SLOs](https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/) — the
+  burn-rate framing this page uses, including why a fast window and a slow one are two rules rather
+  than one rule with two thresholds;
+- [Prometheus: naming](https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/) — the units and the suffixes
+  the family names follow.
